s due to recent infection or stress: atypical for benign processes given the marked elevation and elevated LAP", "Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML): low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase is characteristic, not elevated", "Myelofibrosis: typically presents with constitutional symptoms but shows low LAP and cytopenias, not pancytosis"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Systemic constitutional symptoms (fatigue, abdominal pain)", "Marked leukocytosis (66,500/mm³) with elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.6811449936713742, "posterior": 0.4075988952451248, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Normal differential (no blasts) argues against B2 and favors chronic MPN (B1) as the likely diagnosis."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of splenomegaly and cytopenias is less typical for chronic MPNs but does not decisively exclude them. This weakens B1 slightly."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.088, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal differential (no blasts) argues against B2 and favors chronic MPN (B1) as the likely diagnosis."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.021, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP is a specific finding for chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms (MPNs) but not for CML, which typically shows low LAP. This strengthens B1."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.056, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP with no blasts provides weak evidence for Essential Thrombocythemia (B1.2) over reactive leukemoid reaction (B4), but the normal platelet count re…"]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Polycythemia Vera (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Elevated Hemoglobin and Erythropoiesis)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.051, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The lack of elevated hemoglobin/hematocrit decisively argues against Polycythemia Vera (B1.1), which requires erythrocytosis for diagnosis."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Essential Thrombocythemia (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Persistent Thrombocytosis)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.061, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["A normal platelet count contradicts Essential Thrombocythemia (B1.2), which requires persistent thrombocytosis for diagnosis despite systemic symptoms and elev…"]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (CNL) with Neutrophilic Pancytosis and Elevated LAP", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.269, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of splenomegaly slightly reduces support for Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (B1.3), though it remains consistent with its chronic phase presentation."]}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Chronic Eosinophilic Leukemia (CEL) with Persistent Eosinophilia", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.027, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The complete absence of peripheral eosinophilia directly contradicts Chronic Eosinophilic Leukemia (B1.4), which requires persistent eosinophilia for diagnosis."]}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the marked neutrophilic leukocytosis (66,500/mm³) with elevated LAP and normal differential argue for Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (B1.3) over other myeloproliferative neoplasms like Polycythem…", "summary": "The combination of neutrophilic pancytosis and elevated LAP strongly supports Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (B1.3) over Polycythemia Vera (B1.1) and Essential Thrombocythemia (B1.2), which typically show different cytological patterns."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of systemic constitutional symptoms in a patient with elevated LAP but no blasts support Chronic Eosinophilic Leukemia (B1.4) as a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm?", "summary": "Systemic constitutional symptoms with elevated LAP but no peripheral eosinophilia do not support Chronic Eosinophilic Leukemia (B1.4). The absence of eosinophilia in the differential argues against CEL."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of elevated hemoglobin and hematocrit in a patient with systemic symptoms argue against Polycythemia Vera (B1.1) despite the presence of constitutional symptoms?", "summary": "The lack of elevated hemoglobin/hematocrit decisively argues against Polycythemia Vera (B1.1), which requires erythrocytosis for diagnosis."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the normal platelet count (177,000/mm³) in a patient with systemic constitutional symptoms and elevated LAP inconsistent with Essential Thrombocythemia (B1.2) as the diagnosis?", "summary": "A normal platelet count contradicts Essential Thrombocythemia (B1.2), which requires persistent thrombocytosis for diagnosis despite systemic symptoms and elevated LAP."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of splenomegaly on physical exam argue against Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (B1.3) despite the elevated LAP and neutrophilic leukocytosis?", "summary": "Absence of splenomegaly slightly reduces support for Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (B1.3), though it remains consistent with its chronic phase presentation."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of eosinophilia in the peripheral differential argue against Chronic Eosinophilic Leukemia (B1.4) despite elevated LAP and systemic symptoms?", "summary": "The complete absence of peripheral eosinophilia directly contradicts Chronic Eosinophilic Leukemia (B1.4), which requires persistent eosinophilia for diagnosis."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.604], ["B3", 0.224], ["B2", 0.172]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.681], ["B1.3", 0.238], ["B1.2", 0.195]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.408], ["B1.3", 0.269], ["B2", 0.088]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers present (airway, respiratory, or hemodynamic compromise; altered mental status; uncontrolled hemorrhage). Time-critical conditions (e.g., septic shock, STEMI, stroke) are not indicated by current clinical presentation.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B1) posterior (0.408) < 0.75. No dangerous alternatives with posterior >= 0.10 remain unresolved. Further testing (e.g., bone marrow biopsy, genetic markers) could refine diagnosis."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.26856765809828526, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Leukocytosis', 'neoplasm', 'Decreased eosinophil count', 'leukocytosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.604}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.3", "label": "Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (CNL) with Neutrophilic Pancytosis and Elevated LAP", "posterior": 0.269, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Absence of splenomegaly slightly reduces support for Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (B1.3), though it remains consistent…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B1.2", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Polycythemia Vera (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Elevated Hemoglobin and Erythropoiesis):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, burning sensation in extremities\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Essential Thrombocythemia (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Persistent Thrombocytosis):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (CNL) with Neutrophilic Pancytosis and Elevated LAP:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nPolycythemia Vera (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Elevated Hemoglobin and Erythropoiesis) vs Essential Thrombocythemia (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Persistent Thrombocytosis):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n\nPolycythemia Vera (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Elevated Hemoglobin and Erythropoiesis) vs Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (CNL) with Neutrophilic Pancytosis and Elevated LAP:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nEssential Thrombocythemia (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Persistent Thrombocytosis) vs Chronic Neutrophilic Leukemia (CNL) with Neutrophilic Pancytosis and Elevated LAP:\n  Favours first:  abnormal cerebral vascular morphology, abnormal platelet morphology, abnormal thrombocyte morphology, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology, amaurosis fugax\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Polycythemia Vera (Chronic Myeloid MPN with Elevated Hemoglobin and Erythropoiesis